    "description": {
      "brief": "A simple comparison-based sorting algorithm that repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.",
      
      "detailed": [
        "Algorithm Analysis:",
        "──────────────────",
        "Time Complexity:",
        "• Best case:    O(n)   - when array is already sorted",
        "• Average case: O(n²)  - random data",
        "• Worst case:   O(n²)  - reverse sorted data",
        "",
        "Space Complexity: O(1) - in-place sorting",
        "",
        "Key Characteristics:",
        "• Simple and intuitive implementation",
        "• Excellent for educational purposes",
        "• Efficient for small datasets (< 100 elements)",
        "• Adaptive: can detect already sorted arrays",
        "",
        "Performance Metrics:",
        "• Comparisons: n(n-1)/2 ≈ O(n²)",
        "• Swaps: Up to n(n-1)/2 in worst case",
        "• Memory: Constant O(1) auxiliary space"
      ],
      
      "pseudocode": [
        "void bubble_sort(std::vector<int>& arr)",
        "{",
        "       size_t n = arr.size();",
        "       bool swapped;",
        "",
        "       for (size_t i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i)",
        "       {",
        "               swapped = false;",
        "",
        "               for (size_t j = 0; j < n - i - 1; ++j)",
        "               {",
        "                       if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1])",
        "                       {",
        "                               std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);",
        "                               swapped = true;",
        "                       }",
        "               }",
        "",
        "               // If no swapping occurred, array is sorted",
        "               if (!swapped) break;",
        "       }",
        "}",
        ""
      ],
      
      "optimizations": [
        "Early termination when no swaps occur in a pass",
        "Cocktail shaker sort (bidirectional)",
        "Comb sort (using gap sequences)",
        "Flagged bubble sort to skip sorted portions"
      ],
      
      "use_cases": [
        "Educational purposes and teaching",
        "Small datasets (n < 100)",
        "Nearly sorted arrays",
        "When code simplicity is prioritized over performance"
      ],
      
      "disadvantages": [
        "Very slow for large datasets",
        "Poor cache performance",
        "Inefficient compared to other O(n²) algorithms like insertion sort",
        "Not suitable for production use"
      ],
      
      "related_algorithms": [
